Hypertensive Emergency
Acute Urgent Care
BP >180/120 mmHg, Papilloedema, Altered mental status, Acute end-organ damage

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-036
Reason for Visit: Dangerously high blood pressure readings accompanied by a bursting headache, confusion, and visual changes.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a home blood pressure machine reading over 200 systolic, suffering from a severe headache, nausea, and acute confusion. This constellation of symptoms indicates a hypertensive emergency with active acute end-organ damage (hypertensive encephalopathy). Immediate face-to-face assessment and urgent hospital referral were required to initiate controlled, intravenous antihypertensive therapy and prevent an imminent hemorrhagic stroke or irreversible renal failure, which could not wait for the next daytime clinic.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head feels like it's going to physically explode, and there are blurry grey spots all over my vision.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a rapidly progressive, severe bursting headache that began 6 hours ago and has become unremitting. The spouse reports that the patient has become increasingly lethargic over the past 2 hours, exhibiting profound confusion and repeating questions. The patient complains of significant visual disturbances, describing large scotomata (blind spots) in both eyes. The patient also reports mild shortness of breath and one episode of vomiting. A home blood pressure machine repeatedly showed readings above 210/120 mmHg.
Functional Status: Sitting slumped on the couch, rubbing temples, unable to mobilize due to severe headache and dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of catastrophic intracranial hemorrhage or seizures; spouse is highly distressed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 225/135 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Patient is flushed, visibly distressed by the headache, and exhibiting a sluggish, altered mental status.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam confirms an extremely elevated blood pressure exceeding 180/120 mmHg, taken manually in both arms to rule out dissection. No new murmurs are auscultated, and lungs are currently clear. Neurological exam reveals confusion (GCS 14), but no gross focal neurological deficits (e.g., no hemiparesis). Fundoscopy, while limited in the home setting, strongly suspects the presence of early papilloedema, flame haemorrhages, or cotton wool spots, consistent with malignant hypertension. Expected investigation results would reveal acute end-organ damage, such as an acute rising serum creatinine or acute ischaemic changes on an electrocardiogram.
Environmental Observations: A bowl used for vomiting is next to the couch.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient ceased taking regular Perindopril and Amlodipine two weeks ago because they "felt fine."

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hypertensive Emergency - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to the global nature of the confusion and visual changes (hypertensive encephalopathy) without unilateral focal weakness or classic speech deficits, though urgent CT imaging is required to definitively rule this out.

Migraine with Aura - Ruled out/unlikely due to the critical, extreme elevation in blood pressure (>220 systolic) and the presence of objective confusion/encephalopathy, differentiating it from a benign headache syndrome.

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as the headache built over 6 hours rather than being an instantaneous "thunderclap" onset, but it remains a critical complication of the current hypertension.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the diagnostic tetrad of a true hypertensive emergency: critically elevated blood pressure (>180/120 mmHg), suspected papilloedema/retinopathy, altered mental status, and impending acute end-organ damage. The acute confusion and visual changes confirm hypertensive encephalopathy. An urgent after-hours assessment was critical to confirm the severity of the crisis. Recommending an oral antihypertensive at home would be dangerous, as blood pressure must be lowered smoothly and continuously via intravenous titratable agents in an ICU setting to prevent cerebral hypoperfusion. Immediate 000 activation was mandated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Verified BP manually. Kept patient strictly at rest in a semi-upright position to minimize intracranial pressure while supporting cerebral perfusion. Activated 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport for intravenous antihypertensive management (e.g., Labetalol or GTN infusion).
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your blood pressure medications exactly as prescribed every single day, even when you feel perfectly healthy, you maintain the silent protection that stops your blood vessels from bursting under this kind of massive pressure.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport to a tertiary Emergency Department.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics; requires likely ICU admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The spouse is instructed to keep the patient seated and calm. If the patient begins to seize or loses consciousness entirely, immediately assist them to the floor, lay them on their side, and update the 000 dispatcher.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Your blood pressure is critically high and is putting immense strain on your brain and kidneys. You require emergency intravenous medication at the hospital to bring it down safely.
Clinical Course: The hospital will use an IV drip to slowly lower your pressure by about 25% over the first few hours. Dropping it too fast can actually cause a stroke, which is why it must be done in the hospital.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand up, as this can trigger a sudden drop in blood flow to your brain and cause a faint or a fall. Focus on breathing slowly and calmly.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have not given you an oral blood pressure pill because we cannot control how fast it works. You need an adjustable medication through a vein so the doctors can lower the pressure precisely without starving your brain of oxygen.
Actionable Steps: Let your spouse pack your old medication bottles to show the doctors exactly what you stopped taking. Keep your eyes closed if the lights are worsening your headache.

